WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.240
MCP provides a structured way for AI applications to connect with tools, resources, prompts, data, and services.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:12.260
It helps separate the AI host experience from the integration details of each external system.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:19.600
The core architecture involves hosts, clients, servers, capabilities, context, and interaction flow.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:27.100
Understanding those roles makes it easier to reason about what the AI application can discover, what it can read, what it can call, and what it can change.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.640
Tools, resources, and prompts are different capability types.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.100
Tools perform defined operations.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.680
Resources provide data or content as context.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.700
Prompts provide reusable interaction patterns.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:49.000
Capability discovery lets a client learn what a server offers, but discovery does not automatically mean every capability should be trusted or enabled.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:57.300
Clear descriptions, narrow scope, documentation, and traceability help users and operators understand how capabilities are being used.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.240
Useful MCP adoption depends on identity-aware access and careful data handling.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:11.260
User identity, application identity, service identity, delegated access, consent, least privilege, and tenant boundaries all shape safe use.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:23.480
Prompt data, retrieved resources, tool results, logs, cached content, and memory features need data minimization, provenance, retention rules, and trust-boundary analysis.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:30.340
MCP does not remove the need for application security, authorization, privacy review, or operational discipline.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.720
The goal is to make AI integrations more consistent, understandable, auditable, and maintainable.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:47.100
Good MCP programs define use cases, inventory servers and tools, monitor important events, review dependencies, document ownership, and prepare for incident response.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.640
MCP should make external capabilities easier to govern, not harder to see.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:58.900
When teams treat it as architecture rather than a novelty feature, they can adopt it with clearer control and less operational surprise.
